PriorityQueue is a heap-based queue:

Elements are not stored or retrieved in insertion order

The element with the highest priority (by natural order or a custom Comparator) is always at the head

add() and poll() are O(log n); peek() is O(1)

Duplicates are allowed (unlike Sets)

Backed by a binary heap (min-heap by default)

⚠️ Important: Iterating a PriorityQueue with a for-each loop does NOT give sorted order —
it gives heap-array order. To get sorted output, you must poll() repeatedly

========================================================
Rule of thumb: Reach for PriorityQueue whenever you need to repeatedly extract the "best" element from a changing collection
— best can mean smallest, largest, closest, most urgent, highest-ranked, etc. It's the tool for scheduling, dispatch, triage, and ranking problems.

⚠️ Two things to remember:

PriorityQueue is not thread-safe — use PriorityBlockingQueue in concurrent code.

Iterating a PriorityQueue with for-each gives heap order, not sorted order — always poll() in a loop for sorted output.

================================================================
